# Stimuli-Responsive DNA-Functionalized Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs)

## abstract
The synthesis of nucleic acid-functionalized metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) is described. The metal-organic frameworks are loaded with a dye being locked in the structures by means of stimuli-responsive nucleic acid caps. The pH and K+ -ion-triggered release, and switchable release, are demonstrated.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline porous materials formed by the co-crystallization of metal ions and organic ligands. These materials have attracted substantial research efforts in the past two decades due to their rich compositional and structural diversity as well as the broad possible applications offered by these porous materials.[1–3] The entrapment of guest molecules and nanostructures in the MOF’s high-surface-area matrices has enabled their implementation as nano/ micro reactors for catalytic transformations,[4–6] sensor systems,[7–10] matrices for gas adsorption and air purification,[11,12] and as functional materials for proton conductivity in fuel cell applications.[13–15] In addition, different biomedical applications of MOFs have been suggested, including drug delivery[16] and controlled release of gases.[17]
Substantial recent research efforts are being directed toward the implementation of the functional information encoded in nucleic acids (DNA) to assemble DNA switches[18] and to develop composite stimuli-responsive DNA-based materials. Different DNA-functionalized stimuli-responsive materials have been reported, including nucleic acid-functionalized mesoporous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles,[19,20] DNA-modified hydrogels,[21–24] and nucleic acid-functionalized microcapsules.[25] These have led to functional systems that possess applications in signal-triggered release of loads from different material assemblies, the switchable control of physical and chemical properties of the systems, for example, reversible hydrogel-tosolution transitions,[26,27] and switchable catalytic functions. Within these applications, the stimuli for such reversible switching include pH,[26,28,29] metal-ion/ligands,[30] strand/ anti-strand nucleic acids,[31] and light,[32,33] and have been used to induce changes to the physical and chemical properties of DNA-modified inorganic or organic materials. Interestingly, the conjugation of stimuli-responsive DNAs to MOFs has not been explored, despite the fact that the unlocking of MOFs by chemically modified units has been reported.[34–41] In fact, reports on the integration of nucleic acids with MOFs are scarce, and have been limited to the delivery of DNA.[42] Besides the introduction of stimuli-responsive DNA/MOF hybrids as a new functional material for controlled release and switchable catalysis, the use of MOFs presents important advantages over other stimuliresponsive porous carriers, for example, DNA-modified $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ particles.[20] The surface area of the MOFs is ${\approx}10$ -fold higher as compared to the $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ particles, thus allowing improved loading of the particles.[2] Furthermore, the formation of the MOFs dictates homogenous pore sizes, and thus the release of the loads is anticipated to reveal a more controlled release profile. The conjugation of nucleic acids to MOFs as stimuli-responsive capping units is particularly interesting since biological components such as genes, miRNA, or protein biomarkers may be used to unlock the DNA caps. We note that the results presented in our study highlight the first example of loading/ unloading of MOFs by stimuli-responsive DNA capping units, thus combining the unique structural properties of MOFs with the recognition and catalytic functions of nucleic acids.
In the present study, we report on the design of stimuli-responsive DNA-modified MOFs. We have synthesized pH- and $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ -ion-responsive DNA-functionalized MOFs that demonstrate switchable release of loads entrapped within the MOF structures. We accomplish this through the use of three different DNA switching motifs. Specifically, we implemented the i-motif and triplex DNA nanostructures for developing pHresponsive MOFs, as well as the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ stabilized G-quadruplex as a functional ion-gating unit. Furthermore, by entrapping hemin into the G-quadruplex-functionalized MOFs, we demonstrate the triggered catalytic functions of the hemin/G-quadruplex horseradish peroxidase-mimicking DNAzyme.[43,44] The synthesis of stimuli-responsive DNA/MOF hybrids paves the way to assemble other DNA/MOF hybrids responding to other triggers such as light, temperature, redox reagents, or biomarkers. The properties and functions of such materials are anticipated to have an impact on the development of sensors, new catalysts, photonic devices, and drug delivery carriers.
Figure 1A exemplifies the method to assemble the dyeloaded, DNA-functionalized MOF, which is the technique used throughout our study for each of the stimuli-responsive MOFs through the use of different DNA sequences (Table S1 and Figure S1, Supporting Information). The metal–organic framework selected for the present study is the well-established class of MOFs consisting of zinc-carboxylate clusters bridged by two different organic ligands, amino terephthalic acid and $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -benzene-1,3,5-triyl-tribenzoic acid (BTB).[45,46] The dye rhodamine 6G was used as a model substrate acting as load. Rhodamine 6G exhibits pH-independent fluorescence within our region of interest, specifically $\mathrm{pH}~5.0\substack{-8.0}$ and thus the fluorescence in solution can be used to probe the release of the load. The synthesis of the MOF, and the subsequent loading with rhodamine 6G, is supported by both visual confirmation (Figure S2, Supporting Information) and IR spectroscopy (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The MOFs were synthesized according to the reported literature,[45] and the amine functionalities of the MOF were reacted with succinic anhydride to yield the carboxylic acid functionalities. Subsequent amide coupling of an amine-modified cytosine-rich nucleic acid, (1), to the carboxylic acid-functionalized MOFs yields (1)-functionalized MOFs. By knowing the initial concentration of (1) and the residual concentration of (1) in the solution after modification of the MOFs, we estimate the coverage of (1) to be $3.3~\mathrm{nmol~mg^{-1}}$ MOF. The resulting (1)-modified MOFs were loaded with rhodamine 6G at a concentration of $1.8\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ . The MOFs were acidified at $\mathrm{pH}=5.5$ and then rinsed with an acidic solution (Figure S1A, Supporting Information). The acidification of the system transformed the random coil configuration of (1) into the i-motif structure, a process that locked the rhodamine 6G load in the MOF. Treatment of the (1)-functionalized MOFs at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ resulted in the dissociation of the i-motif units, unlocking the MOFs and leading to the release of the load. Figure 1B depicts this release principle, and Figure 1C, Panel I curve (a), shows the time-dependent release of the dye upon the pH-stimulated unlocking of the i-motif gate units at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ . After ${\approx}60\ \mathrm{min}$ , the complete release of the load is observed. Using an appropriate calibration curve (Figure S4, Supporting Information), we estimate that the loading of rhodamine 6G corresponded to ${\approx}70\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . For comparison, the fluorescence intensities of the solution at $\mathrm{pH}=5.5$ , where the MOFs are locked by the i-motif structures, are unchanged with time, Figure 1C, Panel I curve (b), implying that the dye does not leak out from the locked pores. Figure 1C, Panel II, presents the switchable ON-OFF release of the load upon subjecting the MOFs to cyclic pH changes alternating between $\mathrm{pH}=5.5$ and $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ . As a control, bare MOFs unmodified with DNA were loaded with rhodamine 6G and prepared using the same protocol. The release profile is shown in Figure S5 (Supporting Information), which shows that almost all of the dye was removed in the washing procedure. The pH-stimulated loading and unloading of the MOFs was further supported by Brunauer-Emmet-Teller (BET) surface area measurements, Table S2 (Supporting Information). The unloaded (1)-functionalized MOFs revealed a surface area corresponding to $2860\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and a pore size corresponding to $1.7\mathrm{nm}$ (the nitrogen gas sorption isotherms for the loading and unloading process are shown in Figure S6, Supporting Information). After loading of rhodamine 6G and locking the MOFs with the i-motif units, the surface area of the MOFs decreased to $550~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , consistent with the filling of the MOFs’ pores with the dye. The affinity interactions between the rhodamine 6G and the pores are presumably $\pi{-}\pi$ and H-bond interactions. Upon changing the $\mathrm{\pH}$ of the system to $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ and after release, the surface area of the MOFs increased to $1960\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , consistent with the release of the load from the MOFs. The lower surface area of the MOFs after release, as compared to the MOFs before loading, can be explained by nonspecific interactions between salts and dye molecule that causes retention of a small amount of material even after release. In all of the experiments, we used the MOFs in aqueous media at different pH values. Although previous reports demonstrated the stability of our MOFs in aqueous media of $\mathrm{\pH}$ within the range of our studies,[34] we further examined the surface properties of the MOFs before and after subjecting the MOFs to acidic/ basic conditions. We find that the surface area and pore dimension of the MOFs are almost unchanged (within $\pm10\%$ ) before and after treatment under acidic and basic conditions (the nitrogen gas sorption isotherms for the acidic and basic buffer incubations are shown in Figure S7, Supporting Information). These results, together with the switchable function of the MOFs, imply that the system is stable in the different aqueous environments.
As noted earlier, a similar concept outlined for the synthesis of the (1)-modified MOFs was implemented for the synthesis of the other stimuli-responsive MOFs. For example, Figure S1B (Supporting Information) depicts the synthesis of the (2)/ (3)-modified MOFs that represent a pH-responsive MOF utilizing the ${\mathrm{C}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{G}}{\bullet}{\mathrm{C}}^{+}$ triplex DNA structure as the unlocking motif. The amino-functionalized nucleic acid (2) was conjugated to the carboxylic acid-functionalized MOFs; after this step, the rhodamine 6G was loaded into the MOFs, and the fluorescent dye was then locked in the MOFs by the hybridization of the singlestrand hairpin nucleic acid, (3), with (2). The surface loading of the nucleic acid (2) was measured to be 2.0 nmol $\mathrm{mg^{-1}}$ MOF. The hybrid bifunctional duplex structure (2)/(3) is stable at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ , thus maintaining the large DNA complex at the MOF surface. The base sequence encoded in (3) yields, however, at $\mathrm{pH}=5.5$ , the stabilized ${\mathrm{C}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{G}}{\bullet}{\mathrm{C}}^{+}$ triplex nanostructure[26,27] that separates from the MOF surface. Accordingly, the $\mathrm{\pH}$ stimulated separation of the (2)/(3) hybrid leaves only the short, single strand (2), thus unlocking the MOFs and allowing the release of the load, as depicted in Figure 2A, curve (a). This release is seen in Figure 2B, where at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ , shown by curve (b), only background release is observed, and decreasing the pH to 5.5 results in the unlocking of the nanopores and the release of the fluorescent cargo. After ${\approx}30$ min, the complete release of the load is observed. This shorter time scale is explained by the full release of the triplex sequence into solution, where in the i-motif system the full sequence is still attached through a covalent amide bond, but in a single-stranded form. As opposed to the i-motif system, which demonstrates reversible switching, the kinetics of the triplex switching in this format is not suitable for switching due to the slow recovery of the locked system attributed to the low concentration of the triplex units, (3), in the bulk solution, and thus only triggered release is demonstrated. It should be noted that the (2)-modified MOFs reveal high stability under acidic conditions. The (2)-functionalized MOFs stored at $\mathrm{pH}=5.5$ for a time interval of two months could be reloaded, at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4$ , with rhodamine 6G and locked with (3) to yield particles of identical loading and release profile.
The ion-gated MOF synthesis is presented in Figure 3A, which outlines the switchable release from the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ -ion-stabilized G-quadruplex-locked MOFs. Following the protocol described above, shown in Figure S1C (Supporting Information), aminofunctionalized, guanosine-rich nucleic acid, (4), was coupled to carboxylic acid-modified MOFs. The DNA coverage of the MOF by (4) was estimated to be $1.7~\mathrm{nmol~mg^{-1}}$ . The (4)-modified MOFs were also loaded with rhodamine 6G and were locked by reconfiguring the random coil strands (4) into the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ -stabilized G-quadruplex by addition of $25\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~K^{+}}$ ions. Figure 3B, Panel I curve (a), depicts the triggered release from the G-quadruplex-locked MOFs by the addition of 18-crown-6-ether, which chelates $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions and causes strand (4) to return to a random coil state. Without the presence of 18-crown-6-ether, no release is seen, curve (b). We further demonstrate the switchable unlocking and locking of the MOFs with the concomitant release of the loaded dye molecule. The switchable ON-OFF cyclic release of dye molecule from the MOFs is stimulated by the dissociation of the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ -stabilized G-quadruplex by means of 18-crown-6-ether and the reassembly of the G-quadruplex locking units in the presence of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions. Figure 3B, Panel II, shows this switchable release process, where full release is observed after the final switching with 18-crown-6-ether.
In conclusion, the present study has introduced the novel synthesis of stimuli-responsive DNA-functionalized MOFs. The use of pH or $\mathrm{K^{+}}.$ -ion/18-crown-6-ether triggers has been applied to control the release of loads incorporated in the MOFs. Besides the switchable release of molecular loads, the reconfiguration of the hemin/G-quadruplex led to DNA-modified MOFs exhibiting triggered catalytic functions. The results suggest that other stimuli such as metal-ion/ligand, strand/anti-strand, or light may be applied to trigger the controlled release of loads from MOFs. Furthermore, the controlled unlocking or reconfiguration of the DNA gates and the release of fluorescent loads paves the way toward developing different sensing platforms. Although other stimuli-responsive carriers have been reported, stimuli-responsive DNA-MOF hybrids represent a new material with potential advantages over other particulate porous particles, for example, mesoporous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ particles. The tenfold higher surface area of MOFs as compared to the mesoporous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ and the homogeneity of the pores of the MOFs,[2] dictated by the mode of synthesis, suggest that improved loading and controlled release profiles will be observed with the MOF system. In view of the different stimuli-responsive nucleic acid structures, the development of other DNA/MOFs hybrids being triggered by other external signals such as light, temperature, redox reagents, or biomarkers may be envisaged. Furthermore, the precise steric position of the loads in the MOF pores with respect to the DNA capping units provides a means for effective catalytic cascades between the constituents. Finally, the possibility to miniaturize MOFs into nanostructures provide means to prepare DNA/MOF hybrids, acting as new catalysts, drug carriers, sensors, and photonic devices.
## Experimental Section
MOF Synthesis: MOFs were synthesized by the addition of $487.5~\mathrm{\mg}$ zinc nitrate hexahydrate $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\bullet}6H_{2}O$ , $108\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~\textmu~}$ , $297.49\textrm{g}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1})$ ), $77.0\ m g$ BTB ( $11.7\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~m~}$ , $438.43\textrm{g}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1})$ , and $73.5~\mathsf{m g}$ aminoterephthalic acid $(27\times70^{-3}\mathrm{~\textmu~},$ $187.75\textrm{g}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1})$ in $75m L$ anhydrous dimethylformamide (DMF). The solution was covered and heated for $3{\mathsf{d}}$ at $85~^{\circ}C$ . After crystallization, the solution was washed with DMF for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and then subjected to three anhydrous dichloromethane (DCM) washes of $12\mathrm{~h~}$ each. Modification of the MOF with carboxylic moieties was achieved by incubating ${100}~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{M O F}$ in $5.0~\mathrm{mg~}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ succinic anhydride for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in DMF, washing with DMF for $12\mathsf{h}$ , and then conducting two ${\mathsf{12h}}$ washes in DCM. The MOFs were then dried under reduced pressure for 1 d.
MOF Functionalization: Functionalization with DNA was undertaken by incubating $25\mathrm{~\mg~}$ MOF in $10\ m g\ m L^{-1}$ 1-ethyl-3-(3- dimethylaminopropyl)carbodiimide (EDC, $1m L$ in $1.0\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{m}$ MES, pH 5.5) for 5 min under gentle shaking, followed by addition of $\mathsf{10~m g~m L^{-1}}$ sulfo-N-hydroxysulfosuccinimide (sulfo-NHS), for 15 min under gentle shaking. $2.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of $60\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ DNA ( $10\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}\mathsf{P B S}$ , $\mathsf{p H}~7.4$ for all systems) was then added and the vial was placed under gentle shaking overnight. The DNA-functionalized MOFs were washed in PBS buffer three times to remove unbound DNA. The UV absorbance of the solution at $\lambda=260\ \mathsf{n m}$ was measured to determine the amount of
DNA coating the MOF, using the UV-2450 UV–vis Spectrophotometer (Shimadzu).
MOF Loading: To load the MOFs, $25~{\mathsf{m g}}$ of MOF were incubated with rhodamine 6G $(0.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}})$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ in $4m L$ of buffer solution corresponding to the “opened” form of the DNA-functionalized MOF (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The MOF was then transferred to a buffer solution containing $0.5~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{m L}^{-1}$ rhodamine 6G, but that led to the “closed” state of the DNA to lock the dye inside the pores. After $3h$ the MOFs were washed several times to remove both the solution and nonspecifically bound rhodamine 6G.
Dye Release: For timed release experiments, 1 mL of buffer (corresponding to the “opened” or “closed” state of the stimuliresponsive DNA) was added to $10m g$ of DNA-functionalized, rhodamine 6G-loaded MOF. The vial was gently rotated during release, and $3~\upmu\upiota$ of supernatant were removed for each time point and diluted 200 times before a measurement was taken on the Cary Eclipse Fluorescence Spectrometer (Agilent Technologies). For switchable release experiments, the same measurement procedure was followed, but buffers were now exchanged at the given time points by full removal of the initial buffer (“locking” buffer) and addition of the second (“unlocking” buffer) for the i-motif or addition of either ${\sf K}^{+}$ or 18-crown6-ether for the G-quadruplex.
Catalysis: The catalytic activity of the G-quadruplex-functionalized MOF was measured by making a solution of $2\log$ MOF in $1m L$ HEPES buffer, $\mathsf{p H7}.4$ , containing $25\times70^{-3}\mathrm{~M~NaCl}$ , $25\times\mathsf{7}0^{-3}\mathrm{~M~K^{+}~}$ , $0.5\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{m}$ hemin, ${\sf1}\times{\sf10^{-3}}_{\mathrm{~M~}}{\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ , and $1\times10^{-3}$ m ABTS2−. $20~\upmu\upiota$ of the solution was measured directly at selected time points using the UV-2450 UV–vis Spectrophotometer (Shimadzu).
